WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.266 --> 00:00:05.760
LORRAINE CINK: Marvel
Studios' Moon Knight

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.890
has some otherworldly
action, which

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.370
is why I'm breaking it down
with VFX Supervisor Sean Faden.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.220
Hey, Sean.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.020
 Hey Lorraine.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.270
 Now let's kick
off with episode two

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.070
where we see Mr.
Knight facing off

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.780
against that invisible jackal.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.050
How did you and your team first
start to approach that scene?

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.970
 There was a lot of
weight on this scene,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.370
and making sure
that it landed well.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.320
It actually starts
in the alleyways.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.320
You've got Mr. Knight being sort
of blown through this fence.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.240
We spent a lot of time
pre-vising this scene

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.670
because it was-- he had
the complications of having

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.410
to have an invisible opponent.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.990
The jackal is only
visible occasionally

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:40.863
during the sequence.

00:00:40.863 --> 00:00:42.030
We had a lot of
storyboards that we

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.710
got from one of our main
storyboard artists, Phil

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:45.510
Langone.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.050
It wasn't for every
single sequence,

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.560
but when we did get them,
they were extremely helpful.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:50.880
In the end, how
we shot it was we

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.800
had a stunt person
in a gray suit

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.750
pushing and pulling
our stunt Moon Knight,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.170
or Oscar and Layla around.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.140
For me, the most important
thing to capture for real

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.769
was leaping, or
landing, or running.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.990
We did shoot, when
possible, references.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.870
I think that if you end
up doing too much CG

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.640
throughout every
single shot, you end

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.360
up losing some of the reality.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.010
So we always tried to
shoot with real stunts.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.480
 While we're
talking battles, we

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.210
have to talk about
the end of the series

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.910
where we see your techniques
taken to new heights

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.900
quite literally with a huge
god versus god battle in Cairo.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.210
How did you go about
approaching that scene

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.160
and bringing it to life?

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.890
SEAN FADEN: It was a
combination of a lot of pre-vis,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.140
some storyboarding, a
lot of back and forth

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.840
with our stunt team, trying
to figure out what the best

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.050
flow of fight choreography was
going to be for these moments,

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.840
and making sure that we were
seeing Khonshu getting to be

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.100
heroic and seeing Ammit use some
of her crocodile related fight

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.230
moves.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.910
We actually did a lot of work
within the Chamber of the Gods,

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.800
because we have a scene
that takes place there

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.410
where Khonshu and Ammit are
normal size, which means

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.390
they're actually 9 feet tall.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.160
We had pre-vised all
this stuff, and then

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.080
we went and we shot a
lot of background plates

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.190
within our set, which sometimes
are just looking at a column,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.600
and the rest would
be blue screen.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.060
They created costumes
for Ammit and Khonshu.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.910
Real costumes that moved
like the real thing, that

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.230
look like the real
thing, that could be

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.620
lit like the real thing.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.810
So it wasn't just guys in
gray suits running around.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.160
We actually had a lightable
Khonshu and Ammit in our scene,

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.950
and they were able to
act against each other.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.310
The scale of the two characters
mainly became an issue

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.320
when we were dealing
with other actors that

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.620
needed to interact with them.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.140
So if Oscar was talking to
Khonshu, than Karim, who played

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.050
Khonshu on set, would be
wearing an eyeline pole

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.370
to make him look 9 feet tall.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.700
It was literally
two ping pong balls

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.540
on the end of a composite rod
that was attached to his--

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.990
like a lightweight vest that
our props department made.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.430
 The gods are
already quite tall,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.070
but then they grow even larger.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.530
How did you create this
scene at such an epic scale?

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.440
 So they're almost as
tall as the pyramids,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.870
and they're bashing
around and wreaking havoc.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:12.300
Weta created a full CG Giza
plateau based on some lidar

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.790
that we had gotten
from Disney archives.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.250
We had a lot of work that went
into figuring out just how

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.560
much dust or how much
light should be hitting

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.450
these characters to
make them feel big,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.120
and what we found was the less
light we put on them, the more

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.440
that we sort of played the
light falling off on them,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.250
the bigger they felt.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.920
 This is really just a tiny
drop in the bucket of all

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.630
of the action in this series.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.700
Looking back, are there
any moments that you

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.320
think fans might have missed?

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.190
 The series is jam packed
with lots of cool details.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.260
I don't think you're going to
catch it on the first viewing.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.180
I really think it's one of
those things you have to watch

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.730
a few times to really
appreciate all the detail

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.740
and heart that went
into these moments.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.450
 All right, well I guess
I'm going to have to go

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.160
start my rewatch right now.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.540
So Sean, thank you so
much for talking with us.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.340
 Well, I appreciate it.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.180
Thanks Lorraine.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.250
 And everyone, be
sure to go watch

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.510
Marvel Studios' Moon Knight.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.608
All episodes are now
streaming, only on Disney+.

